 When finally Might Not Execute
 	1.	JVM Shutdown via System.exit(...)
 	•	If you call System.exit(...) in a try or catch, it initiates JVM shutdown, so the finally block might not run.  ￼
 	•	The Oracle Java tutorials explicitly note this.  ￼
 	2.	Runtime.halt (or Abrupt Termination)
 	•	If you call Runtime.getRuntime().halt(...), the JVM halts immediately without giving finally a chance.  ￼
 	•	Also, if the JVM crashes (e.g. due to a fatal error), there’s no guarantee finally will run.  ￼
 	3.	The Thread Is Killed / Interrupted Before Finally
 	•	If the thread running the try / catch is forcefully killed (or the process is killed), finally may never execute.  ￼
 	•	Similarly, if we are in an infinite loop or unbounded recursion in the try block, control may never exit to reach the finally block.


class Person {
    private String id;
    private String name;
    // constructor + getters

    public String getId() { return id; }
    public String getName() { return name; }
}
And you have a List<Person> people that may have duplicate IDs. To create a Map<String, Person> where the key is id and in case of duplicates you ignore the new entry (i.e. keep the first Person seen), do this:

import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.Function;
import java.util.stream.Collectors;

List<Person> people = …;

Map<String, Person> map = people.stream()
    .collect(Collectors.toMap(
        Person::getId,           // key mapper
        Function.identity(),     // value mapper — the Person object itself
        (existing, replacement) -> existing  // merge function: ignore the new Person
    ));￼

Here:
	•	Person::getId picks the key from each object.
	•	Function.identity() returns the object itself as the value.
	•	(existing, replacement) -> existing is the merge function. It says: when there’s a key collision, keep the existing value and discard the replacement.